ITRS Geneos FTM | File Arrival Monitoring on Unix

In production support, one common issue is a file not arriving within the expected time.

For example, a payment or report file is expected on a Unix server every day at 10:00 AM. If the file is delayed, the support team should be alerted before downstream processing is impacted.

This is where the ITRS Geneos File Transfer Monitor, or FTM, can help.

FTM monitors expected file arrivals and checks whether the file is received within the configured time.

It supports:

✅ Expected arrival time  
✅ Daily, weekly, and monthly file schedules  
✅ Monitoring periods  
✅ Specific monitored days  
✅ Holidays  
✅ Late file detection  
✅ Warning or amber status for delayed files  
✅ Critical or red status after the drop-dead time  

Example setup:

Unix server: `appserver01`

File:
/app/inbound/payment_<today>.dat

Expected arrival:
10:00:00

Frequency:
Daily

Monitoring flow:

Unix Server
     |
     | Expected file
     v
/app/inbound/payment_<today>.dat
     |
     v
Geneos Netprobe
     |
     v
FTM Sampler
     |
     v
Check expected arrival
     |
     +-- File received on time → OK
     |
     +-- File received late → WARNING / AMBER
     |
     +-- File not received after drop-dead time → CRITICAL / RED

Basic FTM setup in Geneos Active Console:

Gateway Setup Editor
   |
   +-- Samplers
         |
         +-- New Sampler
               |
               +-- Plugin = FTM

Example sampler configuration:
Name:
payment_file_monitor

Group:
FileMonitoring

Description:
Monitor daily payment file

Sample interval:
20 seconds

Under the FTM Basic configuration, add the file path and expected schedule:

Path:
/app/inbound/payment_<today>.dat

Expected arrival:
10:00:00

Expected period:
Daily

FTM uses the expected arrival time and expected period to determine whether the file has arrived on time.

You can also configure the monitoring period, for example:

Noon_to_noon
Midnight_to_midnight

Specific monitored days can also be configured when the file is expected only on business days:
Monday
Tuesday
Wednesday
Thursday
Friday

Example scenario:

The file is expected at 10:00 AM.

At 10:10 AM:

Status: WARNING / AMBER
Expected: 10:00 AM
Actual: Not received

If the file is still missing after the configured drop-dead time:

Status: CRITICAL / RED

Payment file has not been received
after the expected drop-dead time.

One important prerequisite is that the Netprobe user must have read access to the monitored file and directory.

You can verify the permissions on Unix with:

ls -l /app/inbound/payment_20261007.dat

FTM can be used for monitoring:

📌 Payment files  
📌 SWIFT-related files  
📌 Reconciliation files  
📌 Settlement reports  
📌 End-of-day reports  
📌 Upstream and downstream interface files  
📌 Batch processing input and output files  

Instead of waiting for a business user to report that a file has not arrived, Geneos can detect the delay and alert the support team.

Monitor file arrival → Detect delay → Alert support → Investigate the issue → Reduce business impact

